#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/stabilize-record — the one script through which a stabilization run's records
# reach the repository, and the one place its state is read back and decided
# (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-05, "The stabilization workflow, as ruled": rulings 4, 5, 6,
# 9, 10, 12, 13 and 16; the builders' choices are the two entries of the same date, "The
# record script, as built" and "The record script reads the run back", and the three
# entries of 2026-10-06, "The record script holds what the harness reads its work from",
# "The run's decision table, settled" and "The decision table's holes, closed").
#
# A stage's reporters have a shell and no write tool, and what they write is public on
# the next push and read by fences on the next gate. So neither an agent nor the harness
# writes a report, a ledger row or a clause row by hand: this script decides the path,
# replaces host paths, stops on the public-hygiene scan and writes atomically — or
# refuses in one line and writes nothing.
#
# And the harness is a script with no file system and no shell: it sees what an agent
# returns to it. So the run's state leaves this script as ONE JSON document (state), which
# an agent relays and never compiles; and what follows from that state — whether a finding
# is inside the round's test set, where it is routed, what the round does next — is
# computed here, where a suite holds it to a truth table, and nowhere else.
#
# One run's files, all under completions/artifacts/<run>/:
#
#   opening.md                              the opening record — the human-led step's, never
#                                           this script's
#   run.md                                  the run's facts: its stop mode, the bound across
#                                           rounds, the previous release and the default
#                                           scope                        (run-set)
#   ledger.md                               one row per finding          (ledger-add, ledger-set,
#                                                                         triage-set: the grade)
#   clauses.md                              one row per clause           (clause-set)
#   bounds.md                               one row per declared bound   (bound-set)
#   test-set.md                             one row per instrument item  (item-set)
#   r<N>/scope.md                           the round's doors            (scope-set)
#   r<N>/triage.md                          the round's triage record    (triage-set)
#   r<N>/round.md                           the round record: its facts  (round-set)
#   r<N>/reports/test/<reporter>.a<A>.md    a test-stage report          (report)
#   r<N>/reports/fix/c<K>/<reporter>.a<A>.md  a fix-stage report, cycle K  (report)
#
# A RUN IS OPENED when completions/artifacts/<run>/ is a directory holding a regular
# file opening.md. This script creates neither, so a mistyped run name is refused
# (not-opened) and never mints a second run directory.
#
# Identifiers. <run>, <reporter>, a ledger key, a cited bound, a clause, and a test-set
# item and its kind are slugs
# — a-z0-9 words joined by single `-`, at most 100 characters, the key grammar of
# completions/artifacts/M55/seed-ledger.md. A round, a cycle and an attempt are
# positive integers without a leading zero. Anything else is refused (bad-id) before a
# path is built, and a link at any step below the run directory is refused (outside).
#
# Usage:
#
#   dev/stabilize-record report --run R --round N --stage test|fix [--cycle K]
#                        --reporter S --attempt A [--scratch DIR]... <report
#       The report on stdin, written to the path above; prints that path. --cycle is
#       the fix stage's and only its. A path already taken is refused (exists): a
#       re-run passes the next attempt and the stale report stays where it is.
#       A REPORT ENDS WITH THE LINE
#           <!-- end of report -->
#       as its last line, and it stays in the file. A report without it was cut off on
#       its way — the command it was piped from died — or was never finished, and is
#       refused (truncated): a short text is still text, and nothing else tells the two
#       apart. Input that is JSON needs no such line: cut off, it is not JSON.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record ledger-add --run R [--scratch DIR]... <rows.json
#       One JSON object per finding, or an array of them:
#         key, doctype (jigc-feedback | inconsistency), round (0 is the opening),
#         source, door, clause, repro            — required; the last four are text
#         grade, graded_by, bound, disposition, detail — optional, as for ledger-set
#       A key the ledger already holds is not appended again and keeps its row: a
#       finding found again inherits it. Prints {"ledger", "appended", "existing"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record ledger-set --run R [--scratch DIR]... <patches.json
#       One JSON object per row, or an array: key, and a grade or a disposition.
#         grade        ungraded | breaks | unclear | no-break | out-of-scope
#                      | needs-bound | confirmed | regression | refuted
#         graded_by    triage      gives breaks, unclear, no-break, out-of-scope and
#                                  needs-bound (out of scope as triage reads it, under
#                                  no declared bound: the finding goes to the human)
#                      verify-real gives confirmed, regression (confirmed, and green on
#                                  the previous release), refuted
#                      human       gives any of them; ungraded has no grader
#         bound        with out-of-scope and only with it: the declared bound it cites.
#                      It is a row of bounds.md, or the whole batch is refused
#                      (no-such-bound) — here, in ledger-add and in triage-set.
#         disposition  open | fixed | admitted | bound | later | no-action
#         detail       fixed: the fix's sha · bound: its reach · admitted, later,
#                      no-action: a note, optional · open: none
#       A key the ledger lacks is refused (no-such-row). Prints {"ledger", "updated"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record clause-set --run R --clause C [--instrument TEXT]
#                        [--commit SHA|-] [--scope TEXT] [--status green|red|void]
#                        [--scratch DIR]...
#       Writes the clause's row, or updates the cells named. A new row needs an
#       instrument, a scope and a status. green and red need the commit the instrument
#       ran on; void is "not reached, or could not run", with or without a commit.
#       THE OPENING WRITES ONE ROW PER CLAUSE of the closing condition, each `void` and
#       without a commit: not yet run. A run whose table has no row is not ready (state).
#       Prints {"clauses", "clause", "status"}.
#       NOTHING REMOVES A CLAUSE'S ROW, and nothing removes an item of the test set
#       (item-set). Dropping or replacing either is no ruling inside a round: it changes
#       the closing condition or the test set, and both are fixed by the run's human-led
#       opening before round 1 — a run whose condition changes is opened again. The one
#       ruling a clause that is still not green has a recorded form for is one more re-run
#       of its instrument (round-set: granted).
#
#   dev/stabilize-record bound-set --run R --bound B [--reach TEXT] [--ruling TEXT]
#                        [--pin TEXT] [--scratch DIR]...
#       Writes the declared bound's row, or updates the cells named. A bound is always
#       the human's ruling, so a row enters the list only as the record of one: a new
#       row needs its reach, where the human ruled it (--ruling: the opening record, a
#       stop's list and item), and the test that pins it or the word `unpinned` (--pin).
#       None of the three may be blank. Prints {"bounds", "bound"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record scope-set --run R --round N [--scratch DIR]... <scope.json
#       The round's doors, as the scope step resolved them — one JSON object:
#         {"included": [DOOR...], "excluded": [DOOR...]}
#         DOOR = {"door", "registry", "derivation"}   — text, none of it blank: the door,
#                the registry it comes from, and how it was derived
#       Both sides are named, even when empty, and a door stands on one side, once.
#       Where a whole registry is inside, every door of it is listed. A round's test
#       set is written ONCE: a second scope for the round is refused (exists).
#       Prints {"scope", "included", "excluded"} — the path and the two counts.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record triage-set --run R --round N <entries.json
#       What the round's triage and the verifier established — one JSON object per
#       finding, or an array of them:
#         key          a row of the ledger (a finding is added before it is triaged)
#         grade        triage's: breaks | unclear | no-break | out-of-scope | needs-bound
#         bound        with out-of-scope and only with it: a row of bounds.md
#         verdict      the verifier's, of a finding graded breaks or unclear:
#                      confirmed | refuted. Without one, the finding awaits it.
#         regression   with confirmed and only with it: true when the repro is green on
#                      the previous release's binary — the verifier's fact
#       NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN. Whether the finding is INSIDE the round's test set is
#       computed here — its ledger row's door is one of the round's included doors, as
#       scope-set wrote them — and is never supplied; a round with no scope is refused
#       (no-scope). The entry's row in r<N>/triage.md says: inside | outside: excluded
#       | outside: unlisted, triage's grade, the verdict, the regression, and the
#       disposition the ledger row carried when the round recorded it ("found with").
#       An entry recorded again replaces its row — that is how a verdict arrives later,
#       and how a round says that it found a finding again: only then is it re-recorded.
#       The ledger's grade follows from the same entry, so the two cannot disagree: the
#       verdict where there is one (regression, confirmed, refuted; by verify-real),
#       else triage's grade. A disposition is never touched: a finding found again
#       inherits its row's. Two files, the triage record first: killed between them,
#       the ledger still shows the finding as it was, and the call is simply repeated.
#       Prints {"triage", "ledger", "recorded"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record item-set --run R [--scratch DIR]... <items.json
#       The run's test set, item by item — one JSON object per instrument item, or an
#       array of them:
#         item         the item's id                                   — a slug
#         kind         what kind of instrument it is: a review row, a trial arm, an
#                      audit area, a deterministic check. A slug, and NOT a vocabulary of
#                      this script's: the harness has a chain of agents per kind, and a
#                      kind it has none for is its halt, never a guess of either's.
#         clause       the clause of the closing condition it judges   — a slug
#         runs         every-candidate | in-scope — on every candidate, or only in a
#                      round whose test set reaches it ("hunt once per change, check
#                      always")
#         brief        where its brief lies, or what it is             — text, not blank
#         doors        the doors it covers, each in the door's own words — an array of
#         registries   texts; and the registries it covers whole. Optional, but an
#                      in-scope item names at least one of either, or no round could
#                      ever reach it. An entry is one line, without `<br>`.
#       An item the test set holds is replaced whole: that is how a brief is corrected
#       and how an instrument that was built later takes its row. Prints {"test_set",
#       "written"}.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record round-set --run R --round N <facts.json
#       The round record's facts — one JSON object naming at least one of:
#         candidate    the commit the round tests, a full sha. A round whose record
#                      names its candidate is TESTED: its test stage was recorded.
#         base         the commit the round's change is measured from, a sha
#         binary       the sha256 of the one binary the candidate was driven as
#         cycles       how many fix -> audit cycles of the round are recorded: a
#                      positive integer, which never goes down
#         outcome      dropped | part — the round is OVER without having landed whole:
#                      it was dropped, or a part of it landed. A round that lands whole
#                      needs no word: it is over when nothing of it is open.
#         alone        the clause whose instrument this round ran ALONE, and nothing
#                      else — a slug. It is what puts a clause's one re-run on record
#                      (state: a clause's retry).
#         go           true — THE HUMAN'S GO after the stop that follows this round, in a
#                      run that stops after every round. Taken only while the state's
#                      `next` is `stop`, for that reason and that round; any other time it
#                      is refused (bad-value), so a go never lifts a stop nobody has seen.
#         granted      the clause the human GRANTS ONE MORE RE-RUN, a slug: the clause
#                      whose one re-run this round was, and which is still not green.
#                      Taken only for a clause the state lists in `human_clauses`, in the
#                      round its instrument last ran in; else refused (bad-value).
#                      Both are the human's rulings: the harness writes them in the one
#                      step that records what the human ruled, and nowhere else.
#         cross-model       the items whose source pass was ALSO read by a model of
#         cross-model-void  another family in this round, and the items named for such a
#                      pass that did not get one — each an array of item ids, so that
#                      the use of that pass, and how much of it, shows at every stop
#       candidate, base, binary, outcome, alone, go and granted are written ONCE: a
#       second, different value is refused (exists). NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN, and none of
#       it is free text.
#       Prints {"round", "facts"} — the path, and every fact the record now holds.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record run-set --run R <facts.json
#       The run's facts, as its opening settled them — one JSON object naming at least
#       one of:
#         stop         every-round | at-the-bound — when the run stops for the human of
#                      its own accord: after every round, or only once the bound across
#                      rounds is reached and work remains
#         rounds       the bound across rounds: how many rounds may have a FIX STAGE on
#                      record (state: the stop). A positive integer, which never goes
#                      down. Only the human raises it, and a raise is this call again.
#         previous     the previous release's version (`1.2.3`, `1.2.3-rc.4`), and
#         previous-commit  the commit it was released from, a full sha: what the second
#                      binary of a round is built from, and what "a regression of the run"
#                      is measured against. Each is written ONCE (exists).
#         scope        delta | everything — the scope a round is started with when its
#                      invocation names none
#       THIS IS HOW THE OPENING'S MACHINE-READ FACTS GET IN. opening.md stays the
#       human-led step's prose, which this script neither writes nor reads; the step
#       that writes it calls run-set, and clause-set once per clause of the closing
#       condition, before its one commit. A run that lacks them is NOT READY (state).
#       NOTHING ELSE IS TAKEN, and none of it is free text. Prints {"run", "facts"} —
#       the path, and every fact.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record state --run R
#       The run's committed state, as one line of JSON; writes nothing. A run that was
#       never opened is an answer here, not a refusal: {"run", "opened": false}.
#       Otherwise:
#         run, opened
#         facts        {stop, rounds, previous, previous-commit, scope} as run-set wrote
#                      them (null where it did not)
#         not_ready    what the opening still owes, or []: no-clause-row · no-stop-mode ·
#                      no-round-bound (the mode is at-the-bound, and no number is written)
#                      · no-previous-release · no-default-scope
#         rounds       per round with a directory: {round, scope, triage, record —
#                      whether each file is there — facts: {candidate, base, binary,
#                      cycles, outcome, alone, go, granted, cross-model, cross-model-void}
#                      as round-set wrote them (null where it did not; cycles 0, go false,
#                      the two lists empty), reopened: the keys of the findings this
#                      round's triage found again, standing, with a fix on their row —
#                      the fixes that did not hold, counted where the round's stop shows
#                      them — test_reports, test_attempt, fix_cycles: [{cycle, reports,
#                      attempt}]}, the reports counted by file name and the attempt the
#                      highest one a report's name carries (0 for none)
#         round        the highest of them, or null
#         doors        that round's {included, excluded}, each [{door, registry,
#                      derivation}] — or null before its scope is written
#         bounds       [{bound, reach, ruling, pin}]
#         clauses      [{clause, instrument, commit, scope, status, round, behind, stale,
#                      retry}] — what the row is worth on the current candidate (below)
#         items        [{item, kind, clause, runs, doors, registries, brief, selected}]
#                      — the test set, and whether the latest round runs each item
#                      (below); selected is null for an in-scope item while that round
#                      has no scope
#         ledger       per row: key, doctype, round, source, door, clause, grade,
#                      bound, graded_by, disposition, detail, repro — and
#                        triage  the row's entry in the latest round that triaged it:
#                                {round, inside, door: included | excluded | unlisted,
#                                grade, bound, verdict, regression, found_with}, or null
#                        reopened  whether its fix did not hold: it is open again
#                        route   fix | human | triage | recorded, and `why`
#         blockers     the keys routed fix
#         human_list   [{key, why}] — the findings routed human
#         candidate    {round, commit, current} — the latest round that tested a
#                      candidate, the commit it tested, and whether that candidate is
#                      still the run's: false once a fix stage is on record in that round,
#                      or a later round is begun (null, null, false before any is tested)
#         fix_rounds   how many rounds have a fix stage on record: what the bound counts
#         untriaged    [{key, why}] — the findings routed triage
#         retest       the clauses whose instrument is run again, when that is the step
#         human_clauses  [{clause, why}] — the clauses that are the human's   } once the
#         unsettled    [{cell, clauses}] — what the rulings do not settle      } clause
#                      table is all that is left to ask (below)
#         forbids_close  [{clause, status} | {clause, status, stale} | {"clauses": "no
#                      row"} | {finding, route} | {"candidate": "not-tested", round}]
#         next         not-ready | test | triage | rule | fix | retest | close | stop
#                      | unsettled
#         stop         null — or, when next is stop, {why, round, then}
#         position     {test, fix} — what an invocation of each stage would work on
#                      (below)
#       A cell is given as the table holds it, a `|` unescaped; `-` for none is null
#       where the cell is not free text. A table that is not this script's, a cell
#       outside its vocabulary, or a triage record that disagrees with the doors it was
#       computed from is refused (corrupt) — the state is never read on a guess.
#
#       THE ROUTE of a finding, a pure function of its row, the round's doors and the
#       bounds list — by its disposition first, then by its grade:
#         fixed                  recorded (fixed) — unless the latest round that triaged
#                                it found it again, standing, with that same fix on its
#                                row. The fix did not hold: the row does not inherit
#                                `fixed`, the finding is OPEN AGAIN (reopened) and is
#                                routed by its grade, as below. A later fix, or the
#                                human's ruling, is what records it.
#         bound, later           recorded (ruled): it carries the human's ruling
#         admitted               fix (admitted): the human admitted it to this run
#         open, no-action        by the grade — no-action is neither a fix nor a ruling:
#           ungraded             triage (ungraded)   — the round's triage is not
#           breaks, unclear      triage (unverified)   finished: never a fixer's, and
#                                never on the human's list as if it were verified
#           no-break, refuted    recorded (not-a-break)
#           out-of-scope         recorded (out-of-scope) while its bound is on the
#                                list; human (bound-not-listed) when it is not
#           needs-bound          human (needs-bound)
#           regression           fix (regression) — wherever it was found
#           confirmed            fix (inside) when the latest round that triaged it
#                                has its door among the included; human (outside)
#                                when not; human (untriaged) when no round placed it
#
#       THE CANDIDATE, AND WHEN A FIX STAGE IS ON RECORD. A round whose record names its
#       candidate is TESTED. A FIX STAGE IS ON RECORD in a round whose record counts a
#       fix -> audit cycle, or says how the fix stage ended it (its outcome). The
#       candidate the latest round tested is CURRENT — the run's own — for as long as no
#       fix stage is on record in that round; after that, what the round leaves is tested
#       by nobody until the next round's `test` stage is recorded. CLOSE IS REACHABLE ONLY
#       FROM A TESTED CANDIDATE: from a latest round that is tested, has no fix stage on
#       record, and left nothing open. (A dropped round lands nothing; it is read like
#       the others — the latest stage on record is a fix stage, and one more `test` round
#       is what confirms the candidate. A round's record does not say that its branch was
#       merged, only that a cycle was recorded: the state on a round's own branch reads
#       like the state after its landing.)
#
#       WHAT A CLAUSE'S ROW IS WORTH ON THE CURRENT CANDIDATE.
#         round    the round its instrument LAST RAN in: the highest round whose
#                  candidate is the commit its row names — or null
#         behind   how far behind the current candidate its evidence is, IN FIX ROUNDS:
#                  how many rounds, from `round` on, have a fix stage on record. 0: no
#                  fix stage since it ran. null where `round` is. (The unit is the one the
#                  committed record supports. In commits it is `git rev-list --count
#                  <commit>..<tip>`, which this script cannot say: it reads no git.)
#         stale    for a green row, why it does NOT COUNT on the current candidate — or
#                  null when it does, and for a row that is not green:
#                    no-round      its commit is one no round tested
#                  — and where `behind` is above 0, "hunt once per change, check always":
#                    check-always  an item that runs on every candidate judges the
#                                  clause: a deterministic check is evidence about the
#                                  candidate it ran on, and about no later one
#                    not-scoped    only in-scope items judge it, and a fix round since
#                                  is not followed by a round that resolved a scope of
#                                  its own (a round that ran one clause's instrument
#                                  alone names the round before's doors again): nobody
#                                  has said which doors those fixes reach
#                    reached       such a round's included doors reach one of its items
#                                  (as `selected` is computed, below), and its row is
#                                  not from that round
#                    no-instrument no item of the test set judges it
#                  A hunt's green from before a fix round COUNTS when every fix round
#                  since was followed by a round with a scope of its own and none of
#                  those scopes reaches an item of the clause.
#         retry    null for a green row that counts. Any other clause is owed ONE more
#                  run of its instrument, alone:
#                    no-instrument  no item judges the clause: nothing can be run again
#                    spent          its row is not green, and the round it last ran in is
#                                   one whose record says `alone: <that clause>` and not
#                                   `granted: <that clause>` — its one re-run, or the one
#                                   more the human granted, was that round
#                    due            otherwise: a row never run, a row not green that a
#                                   round ran among others, a green row that is stale
#
#       THE NEXT STEP, a pure function of the routes, the clause table, the rounds'
#       records and the run's facts. A finding is OPEN while it is not routed recorded.
#       The latest round R is OVER when it is tested and its outcome is set or nothing is
#       open. The first that holds:
#         not-ready  the opening owes something (not_ready), and no stage starts
#         test       there is no round, or R is not tested: its `test` stage is run, or
#                    run again — or R is over and a fix stage is on record in it (its
#                    fixes landed, a part of it landed, or it was dropped): the next
#                    round's `test` stage comes first, WHATEVER THE CLAUSE TABLE SAYS
#         triage     a finding is routed triage: the round's triage is not finished. The
#                    stage that left it finishes it (the position, below) — grading,
#                    verification and the record, and no instrument run again
#         rule       a finding is routed human: the human's list has an unruled item
#         fix        a finding is routed fix
#       — and with nothing open, on a current candidate, by the clause table:
#         rule       a clause's retry is spent (human_clauses): the human may grant it
#                    one more re-run (round-set: granted), and no other ruling on a
#                    clause has a recorded form
#         unsettled  a clause's retry is `no-instrument`
#         retest     a clause's retry is due: its instrument is run again, alone, in a
#                    round of its own. `retest` names every such clause; a round runs one.
#         close      every clause row is green and counts
#       THE STOP. The step above can be the human's first: `next` is then `stop`, and
#       `stop.then` is the step that follows the human's go.
#         every-round  the run's stop mode is every-round, R is over — whatever the step
#                      is — and R's record holds no `go`. THE HUMAN'S GO IS A RECORDED
#                      FACT (round-set: go): once it is written, `next` answers the step.
#         round-bound  the mode is at-the-bound, the step is `fix`, no fix stage is on
#                      record in R yet, and as many rounds have one as the bound allows
#                      (fix_rounds >= facts.rounds): one more fix round would start here.
#                      THE BOUND COUNTS FIX ROUNDS, NEVER TEST STAGES: the `test` stage
#                      after a landed round is always allowed — the last allowed fix round
#                      could not be confirmed otherwise — and so are a finished triage,
#                      the human's ruling on the list, and a clause's re-run. Only the
#                      human raises the bound, and the raise is the go (run-set).
#       Close is forbidden by every row that is not green or does not count, by a table
#       with no row, by every finding not routed recorded, and by a candidate that is
#       not tested — forbids_close lists them all.
#       UNSETTLED is not a decision: it names a state the recorded rulings do not
#       settle, and hands it to the human instead of guessing. The cells left, both with
#       nothing open on a current candidate: not-green-and-no-instrument (a clause whose
#       row is not green, and no item of the test set judges it) and
#       stale-and-no-instrument (a clause whose green row is from before a fix round,
#       and no item judges it): there is no instrument to run again.
#
#       WHETHER THE LATEST ROUND RUNS AN ITEM, a pure function of the item's row and
#       that round's doors: an every-candidate item is selected, always; an in-scope
#       item is selected when one of the round's INCLUDED doors is one of its doors, or
#       comes from one of its registries — matched by the cell's text, exactly, as a
#       finding's door is.
#
#       THE POSITION, a pure function of the rounds' records, the routes, what the
#       opening owes and the stop — the round, the cycle and the attempt are read here
#       and are never an argument of anybody's. With R the latest round:
#         the run is not ready   test refused · fix refused (not-ready)
#         no round yet           test {round 1, attempt 1} · fix refused (no-round)
#         R is not tested        test {round R, attempt: the highest test attempt on
#                                disk + 1} — a test stage that never reached its record
#                                is run again, as the next attempt
#                                · fix refused (not-tested)
#         R's outcome is set     test {round R+1, attempt 1} · fix refused (round-over)
#         R is tested, a finding test refused (round-open): fix it, rule on it, or drop
#         is open                the round · fix {round R, cycle: its recorded cycles
#                                + 1, attempt: the highest attempt of that cycle on
#                                disk + 1, land: false}
#                                — and where a finding is routed triage, THE STAGE THAT
#                                LEFT THE TRIAGE UNFINISHED carries `triage: true`: the
#                                fix stage (as above) when one has left a counted cycle
#                                or a report in R; else test {round R, attempt: the
#                                highest test attempt on disk + 1, triage: true} in
#                                place of its refusal. An invocation with `triage: true`
#                                finishes the round's triage and runs no instrument.
#         R is tested, nothing   test {round R+1, attempt 1} · fix {round R, cycle,
#         is open                attempt as above, land: whether a cycle is recorded} —
#                                the round's fix diff was audited and left nothing open
#       Where the table says test {round R+1} and `next` is `stop` because the run stops
#       after every round, test is refused (stopped) instead: round R+1 waits for the
#       human's go. Where it says fix {…} and `next` is `stop` because the bound is spent,
#       fix is refused (round-bound): one more fix round is the human's to allow. A
#       refusal is {"refused": WORD, "round": R}. The state is read from the working
#       tree, so it is the checked-out branch's: a fix stage's records ride its round
#       branch until the round lands.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record check-reports --run R --round N --stage test|fix [--cycle K]
#                        --attempt A [--] [REPORTER...]
#       Exactly one report of that attempt per launched reporter. Prints
#       {"check": "reports", "ok", "dir", "attempt", "missing", "extra",
#       "other_attempts"}: a launched reporter with no report is missing; anything else
#       in the stage's directory is extra, by file name — a report nobody launched, a
#       stray file, the temporary of a killed write. Both fail. Reports of other
#       attempts are listed and decide nothing.
#
#   dev/stabilize-record check-ledger --run R [--] [KEY...]
#       Exactly one ledger row per finding id. Prints {"check": "ledger", "ok",
#       "missing", "duplicated"}.
#
# All of a batch is written, or none of it, and a table has one writer at a time: the
# table writers queue on the run directory's lock, so rows written at once are all
# written — a lock taken once a writer has its input, never while it waits for it — and
# state reads between two of them, never across one. Every JSON result is one line on
# stdout.
#
# What a writer does to its text, in order — every writer alike, because every write
# goes through one function:
#
#   1. Host paths become the public placeholders. A path into this repository becomes
#      its repository-relative form (`.` for the root); a path under a --scratch root
#      becomes <scratch>/…; under the system temp directory ($TMPDIR, /tmp, /var/tmp,
#      macOS's /var/folders/*/*/T), <tmp>/…; under a home directory (this machine's,
#      and any /Users/<name> or /home/<name>), ~/…. The longest root wins. A session's
#      scratch area nobody declared still names the home directory, flattened into a
#      path segment, after that: it is refused (host-path) with what to pass.
#   2. In a table, a cell cannot break its row: `|` is escaped, a line break becomes
#      <br>, an empty cell is `-`.
#   3. No file that would redden a standing fence. One fence reads every markdown file,
#      untracked ones included — install_line (crates/cli/tests/install_line.rs): a
#      file with a line that starts `cargo install jigc` is refused (fence), naming it.
#      Reports stay markdown; write the command inside a line, or behind a `$ `.
#   4. The public-hygiene scan, hard (implementation/public-hygiene.md). The file is
#      committed at its own path into a throwaway repository, and the two scanners CI
#      runs over a push run over that commit: dev/hygiene-scan, unmodified, with the
#      private denylist ($JIGC_DENYLIST_FILE, else ~/.config/jigc/denylist), and
#      gitleaks with this repository's .gitleaks.toml. A hit is refused (hygiene), by
#      where it is and never by what matched. A scanner that could not run — no
#      denylist, no gitleaks, a pattern no matcher accepts, a scanner error — DID NOT
#      RUN (did-not-run): that is never a pass, and nothing is written.
#   5. The write is atomic: a temporary beside the target, then a link (a report, which
#      never replaces a file) or a rename (a table). A run killed at any point leaves
#      no partial file at the target.
#
# Exit status, and the word a refusal opens with — `stabilize-record: refused <word>:
# …`, one line on stderr, nothing on stdout:
#
#    0  written, the check holds, or the state is printed
#    2  usage         an unknown subcommand or flag, a missing argument
#    3  not-opened    the run has no opening record
#    4  bad-id        an identifier outside its grammar
#    5  outside       a link below the run directory: the write would land outside it
#    6  exists        the report's path is taken — pass the next attempt; or a round's
#                     scope, a fact of its record, or the run's previous release is
#                     written once and stands
#    7  bad-value     a value outside its vocabulary, a malformed row, a report that is
#                     not text — or a go, or one more re-run of a clause, that the state
#                     does not ask the human for
#    8  no-such-row   ledger-set names a key the ledger lacks
#    9  fence         the file would redden a standing fence, which is named
#   10  host-path     a host path the placeholders could not replace
#   11  hygiene       the public-hygiene scan found a hit
#   12  did-not-run   a scanner could not run
#   13  corrupt       a table is not the one this script writes, or holds a cell it
#                     does not write
#   14  no-such-bound out-of-scope cites a bound the declared-bounds list lacks
#   15  no-scope      triage-set names a round whose scope was never written
#   16  truncated     the report does not end with its last line: it was cut off
#   20  check-reports failed: a report is missing or extra (the result is on stdout)
#   21  check-ledger  failed: a row is missing or duplicated (the result is on stdout)
#
# Needs python3, git and bash (dev/hygiene-scan), which the gate needs already, and
# gitleaks and a denylist to write anything at all.
# crates/cli/tests/dev_stabilize_record.rs drives it.

